WORKSPACE
Privacy and data policy

What Hive's API gateway stores, where a request goes once it leaves the gateway, and what is not yet a real control. Every statement below is backed by something verified in code, not a policy promise layered on top.

Request and response content

Scoped to requests made with your API keys through Hive's gateway. This page does not describe Hive Chat's own conversation storage, which is a separate system.

The metering record Hive keeps per request carries token counts, cost, status, model alias, endpoint, error codes and operational metadata. Message content is stripped from that metadata before the record is written, so your prompts and completions are not part of it.

No synchronous request through this gateway has its request or response body stored: chat completion, completion, embedding and audio requests are relayed and not retained. One exception, named because it is a real one: when an upstream provider fails a request, the error text it returned is written to this deployment's server logs for diagnosis, and a provider that echoes part of a request in its error text would have that fragment logged with it.

Three endpoints on this same gateway do store content, because storing it is what they are for. If you use them, the content below is held on your account until you delete it:

Batch jobs. The input file you upload holds your request bodies verbatim, and the job writes an output file holding the upstream responses verbatim. Both are stored as files on your account.
File uploads. Anything uploaded through the files API is stored until you delete it through that same API.
RAG documents. Indexed documents are stored in full, both as the original upload and as the text chunks the retrieval index searches. Deleting a document deletes its chunks with it.

There is no automatic deletion schedule for any of this. The per-request metering record is retained indefinitely for billing and audit unless an operator removes it at the database level, and stored content stays until you delete it.

Error messages returned by the API are sanitized before they reach you: which upstream provider served or failed a request is never named in an error response, by design and enforced in tests.

View your usage log
Where a request goes

